{:title
 "Infrastructure in Smile, the Parser, Watcher, Carrier and Ledger Beneath the Language",
 :description
 "What the infrastructure is in THE MAP of Smile, a parser, dresser, assembler, compiler view, watcher, carrier, runner, ledger and projector that know zero meaning and carry what is written exactly, and how my words reached it, from the open source toolset promised in July 2025 through the compiler of November 2025 to the watcher of August 2026, with every source.",
 :keywords
 "prompt compiler, prompt tooling, prompt infrastructure, watcher, parser, version-controlled prompts, Smile prompt language, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, the infrastructure is what carries Smile without knowing its meaning, a parser, a dresser, an assembler, a compiler view, a watcher, a carrier, a runner, a ledger and a projector.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP gives each its work. The parser reads any smiling face in the version's characters. The assembler resolves positions, nesting, variables and conditions. The watcher is to run continuously, composing everything within a second of any save. The carrier writes each render to its destinations and carries an edit made inside a render back to the piece that wrote the words. The runner sends, waits, returns responses and writes every response acted on into the ledger, the one append-only record every reader reads from. The infrastructure knows zero meaning and carries what is written to its readers exactly, and it differs per interface, Claude Code, a web chat or an API."
 "In July 2025 the infrastructure was a promise made to contributors. The Claude Smile document wrote, “The LLM becomes a programmable interface.”, and “just like Markdown or YAML, Smile does not require tooling—but we’re building the tools anyway.”[[m:2025-07-14]] The V.3 Smile Expert said, “This is not speculative. It is real, human-readable, version-controllable prompt infrastructure.”[[m:2025-07-14#2]]"
 "In November 2025 I set what the first compiler must do, with prompts kept as files. On 6 November, “This GUI interfaces into version controlled prompts in folders.”[[m:2025-11-06#2]] On 9 November, “I don't want any of the text changed at all. In the compiler it needs to throw an error every time that it meets something it doesn't recognize”, and “I want you to create compiled files in .txt form with the same file name as the .smile file”.[[m:2025-11-09]] On 11 November, “Every smile has 3 files , .smile which includes var links, .txt is the compiled replaced text output, and .md is the explainer for that text”.[[m:2025-11-11]] In December I chose Clojure, “i figure i use clojure cause the dev i know uses it so we can collab.”[[m:2025-12-07]]"
 "In July and August 2026 the compiler became the watcher. On 22 July I asked, “how do i make it so im always writing the exact thing the model sees, and some web interface isnt a middleman”.[[m:2026-07-22]] On 26 July, “we are also making the compiler another window running all the time”.[[m:2026-07-26]] On 5 August, “a compiler integrated into the watcher thats there (Smile Grow) that is always compiling modules and compositions”,[[m:2026-08-05]] and on 7 August, “we also want every smile to be compiled into a text file all the time.”[[m:2026-08-07]] In September and October the tools were to be reused and scripted, “It's really essential that 99% of the processing is scripted and already pre-written.”,[[m:2026-09-28#2]] and “Always use babashka for scripting instead of python / node / bash etc”.[[m:2026-10-02]]"
 "So the infrastructure moved from a toolset promised to contributors in 2025, through a compiler asked to refuse what it does not recognise, to a watcher that is to compose everything within a second of a save and is still part built, across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes from {{first}} to {{last}}. The ledger it writes to is followed in [[t:memory]], and the linter beside it in [[t:integrity]]."],
